The code statements that lead to the bug are:
rqst[0].rq_iov = open_iov;
rqst[1].rq_iov = &close_iov;
smb2_set_related(&rqst[1]);
Reason:
The iov arrays `open_iov` and `close_iov` are not initialised before being assigned to the request structures. If `SMB2_open_init()` or `SMB2_close_init()` fails (e.g., due to a reconnect), the subsequent calls to `SMB2_open_free()`, `SMB2_close_free()`, or `smb2_set_related()` operate on uninitialised memory, causing a kernel oops.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
+	memset(open_iov, 0, sizeof(open_iov));
+	memset(&close_iov, 0, sizeof(close_iov));
+	smb2_set_related(&rqst[1]);
Reason:
Zeroing `open_iov` and `close_iov` ensures that all iov fields are safely initialised, so even if the initialization functions fail, the cleanup code will operate on valid zeroed memory instead of uninitialised data. Additionally, moving `smb2_set_related(&rqst[1])` to after the successful return of `SMB2_close_init()` prevents it from being called when `close_iov` might not have been properly set up.

